A||||9||SLYGUY||ECU diagnosis...||||5||
Z||000000||SLYGUY||09-16-2001||07:10 PM||||My 88 GT is having some sensor problems. First few days that I had it I noticed that the idle tended to lope a little. At it's worst, it will lope around 3-400 rpm. <BR>Another thing I noticed was that if the car sat for longer than half an hour that blackish sooty smoke would flow from the exhaust on startup for about 2-3 seconds.<P>I figured that the mixture was wrong and that the ECU wassn't getting an accurate reading of it at idle, hence the lope. This would also explain why the mixture would be wrong on startup. <P>I looked in the Haynes manual that I have and it gives you a thorough step by step process to put the ECU into diagnostic. You ground out two pins in the diagnosis cable just underneath the cigarette lighter plate. First he ECU tells you that it is working properly, then displays the stored codes with series of flashes of the "Service Engine Soon" light. <P>Did that, it worked and displayed a code for the EGR valve. I'll go ahead and replace it, but what does this do for the motor? How does this affect mixture, smoking on startup, timing, and what will happen after I change it?<P>Your help is appreciated as always,<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||SLYGUY||09-17-2001||01:31 AM||||Geez.... and I thought everyone here was helpful.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Sly<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||09-17-2001||03:08 AM||||Patience, my friend.<P>You can test the EGR valve using a vacuum tester, like a Mity-Vac.  Hook the Mity-Vac up to the vacuum line on the EGR valve, and pump it up.  If it has any effect on the idle speed then, the diaphragm on the EGR is intact.  Doesn't mean the valve itself is working, though.  Fiero V-6's have an EGR vacuum control solenoid, mounted on a pedestal just to the right of the intake manifold water outlet neck.  It's about 2" around, 4 " long, and has a big flexible vacuum line coming in from forward, and some hard plastic lines going out, plus a connector plug.  These go bad, and will set an EGR code (can't remember the exact code of the top of my head, but it's in the Haynes manual).  These go bad from water, due to the location (under the vent) and in my case, getting a little too wild at the car wash with a hose.  Cost me $90 at the dealer, back in 94 (nobody else around sold them for Fieros).<BR>EGR sends a portion of the exhaust back into the intake, to cool the actual combustion temp (probably chemically, best I can guess), to minimize emissions (NOx, I think).<BR>The black smoke on startup is common to any high mileage engine.  Valve stem seals get old and leak.  I replaced mine on my 161,000 mile 2.8, and no more smoke on startup.  The mosquitos are making a comeback in my neighborhood now.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||09-17-2001||03:10 AM||||Try this link, too.<P>Fiero Online Service Guide.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||SLYGUY||09-17-2001||12:27 PM||||Thanks so much for the info.<BR>Knew I could count on you guys. <P>Not looking forward to have that valve stem work done to cure the occasional smoke plume though. Hopefully at 101K my 2.8 is not old enough yet to be doing this. Using a thinner/thicker oil help this situation?<P>There is never any oil based BLUE oil so I think that narrows it down to a fuel mixture setting. Hopefully.<BR>Keep you guys posted.<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Turbo88||09-17-2001||05:09 PM||||It might also be that you have a leaky injector or a weak link in your ignition system. If you are having fuel puddle into the flow of the intake when your car is off, it will cause that black smoke (usually an excessively rich mixture). It is also possible that you are getting a sporadic miss (can cause the lopy idle). To make matters worse, the first problem might have fouled your plugs causing the second problem. One suggestion is to pull your plugs and see if they are fouled (my pitties for us V6ers). I believe the V6 Fiero has a cold start injector that sprays extra fuel into the intake to richen up the mixture on startup (rich mixtures are easier to ignite). You might want to look at that system too. Just some thoughts...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||09-17-2001||08:01 PM||||I'd definitely make sure the engine is tuned up before I tore into it any deeper.  Plugs and wires take under a half hour, with practice and a good set of Mechanix Wear gloves.  Even esier with the deck lid off.  Cap, rotor, wires, plugs, pickup coil all need to be in good shape. And no vacuum leaks.  I think the MPFI we have is very forgiving, since it is batch fire, vs. sequential.  A leaky injector would probably only show up by looking at the individual plugs, and maybe poor fuel mileage.<P>I lived with the oil burning smoky startups for the simple fact that it was only a pint or so between oil changes.  The seals can be replaced (supposedly) without pulling the heads, provided you have the proper spring compressor tools and a LOT of patience.  I only changed mine because the heads were off anyway, to fix a broken exhaust manifold bolt.  A real PITA.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SLYGUY||09-18-2001||03:23 AM||||After I took a little closer of a look, I've noticed that the wisp of smoke is actually grey in color. It's not puffy and white. I've seen a car running with a blown head gasket. It barely ran and it looked more like steam coming out of the tailpipe than exhaust. Water dripped from the tip too. <P>I've only had the chance to change all the fluids and filters. Took a look at one of the plugs and it looked crappy. I'm hoping that changing the cap and rotor, plugs, and faulty EGR valve will cure this stuff.<P><BR>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000008||SLYGUY||09-18-2001||03:47 AM||||Not to beat an old horse before I dig in and change the plugs and EGR, but if it were indeed a head gasket leak would it steam continuously and make the oil milky?<P>Neither of the above has happened I'm just trying to rule some stuff out so that if it still does this after I do the above mentioned work I will know better whats up.<BR>Sly<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||SLYGUY||09-18-2001||03:49 AM||||One more thing. <P>After even a short run there are small particles of soot on the rear bumper and within a few days there are black stains above the tips of the exhaust.<P>Sly||||reg||1
